Answer: No, audits and recounts conducted after the 2020 election in contested states did not reveal evidence of widespread "phony ballots" that changed the outcome. As reported by PBS and FRONTLINE, a detailed analysis of the Heritage Foundation's Election Fraud Database—cited by proponents of fraud claims—found "no credible threat to the 2020 election" and stated that the database "does not include a single example of a concerted effort to use absentee ballot fraud to steal a major election, much less a presidential election."

Source URL: https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/frontline/article/heres-why-concerns-about-absentee-ballot-fraud-are-overhyped/

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the fact-check reveals that as of October 30, 2020, there was no documented evidence of widespread fraudulent ("phony") ballots being submitted in the 2020 US presidential election; studies and expert statements consistently indicated that voter fraud is rare. The claim's reference to "cheaters" was directed at Democrats and proponents of mail-in voting, but no coordinated plan to commit fraud or delay the count was found—FBI Director Christopher Wray explicitly stated there was no historical evidence of such coordination. The extended timelines for counting mail-in ballots were attributed to state laws in Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, and Michigan that prohibited early processing, not fraud, with election officials from both parties confirming delays due to high volumes of legally cast ballots. Courts extensively investigated fraud allegations and dismissed them as lacking merit, while non-partisan experts like the Bipartisan Policy Center warned that slow counts could lead to unfounded fraud claims. Historical precedent shows that counting legitimate mail-in ballots often continues after Election Day due to varying state deadlines, and audits or recounts did not reveal "phony ballots" altering the outcome.

The evidence is comprehensive and consistent, leaving no substantial gaps in information to require further investigation. All sources—from legal rulings, expert analyses, and historical records—directly contradict the claim's assertions of fraud and intentional delays.

In conclusion, the fact-check explicitly refutes the claim that fraudulent ballots were submitted and that delays were due to cheating, as the evidence demonstrates delays were caused by legitimate state laws and no instances of coordinated fraud were found. Therefore, the decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check found no evidence of widespread fraudulent ballots in the 2020 election, as studies and [expert statements](https://www.bbc.com/news/54562611) consistently indicated voter fraud is rare. The extended counting timelines were attributed to [state laws in Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, and Michigan](https://www.cnn.com/interactive/2020/politics/mail-in-voting/) that prohibited early processing of mail-in ballots, not fraud, with [election officials from both parties](https://bipartisanpolicy.org/report/counting-the-vote-during-the-2020-election/) confirming delays were due to high volumes of legally cast ballots. Courts extensively investigated fraud allegations and [dismissed them as lacking merit](https://www.forbes.com/sites/alisondurkee/2020/10/23/trump-mail-in-voter-fraud-claims-have-no-merit-courts-rule-new-jersey/), while [audits and recounts](https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/frontline/article/heres-why-concerns-about-absentee-ballot-fraud-are-overhyped/) revealed no "phony ballots" altering outcomes. Historical precedent shows counting legitimate mail-in ballots often [continues after Election Day](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/30/fact-check-some-mail-ballots-arriving-post-election-day-count/6075311002/) due to varying state deadlines.